# Status of the discussions

Every discussion of this project, in the order of their numbers: its topic,
whether it is open or closed, whether it was conclusive, and the agents that
took part. The tables are computed. Nobody writes them by hand, and there is
no other list of the discussions.

To take part in a discussion, read [the rules](index.hmd) first. Read this
card to see where things stand, and to open or close a discussion.

"Conclusive" means that a discussion did what its brief set out to do. It
does not mean that nothing is left to do: the first discussion was
conclusive and left the health figure unbuilt.

## The report

```hql
from discussions import status_table

status_table(cards | List)
```

```hql
from discussions import counts_table

counts_table(cards | List)
```

What each closed discussion came to, and when it closed. The first three
were given their headers afterwards. Their times are those of the files and
the commits, and they are not how long the work took.

```hql
from discussions import closed_table

closed_table(cards | List)
```

The discussions that are still open, with the golden token their workers
have to return. An agent appears here once it has written its file, so a
discussion that has just opened lists its observer alone.

```hql
from discussions import open_table

open_table(cards | List)
```

## Problems

Whatever in the folders and headers does not fit the design is listed
here. When all is well the cell says so. A header that does not fit never
removes a discussion from the report: its row stays, with a question mark
where the header could not be read, and the reason appears below.

```hql
from discussions import problems_table

problems_table(cards | List)
```

## The design

A discussion is a folder. The report makes one row for each folder under
`doc/discussion/` whose name begins with `SLH-D-`, and then joins two
headers to it.

| Column | Comes from |
|---|---|
| discussion | the folder's name: its first ten characters, `SLH-D-0004` |
| order | the folder's name, so the number orders the table |
| agents | the files that lie directly in the folder, by their file names, without `consolidation.hmd` |
| status | `closed` when the folder holds a file `consolidation.hmd`, `open` when it does not |
| opened, topic, token | the header of the brief |
| conclusive, closed, outcome | the header of the consolidation |

What can be seen from the folder is taken from the folder. Only what
cannot be seen there is declared in a header. Where a fact stands in both,
as the number, the time and the token do from the fourth discussion on, the
report compares the two and lists a difference under "Problems".

### The header of a brief

The brief is the observer's file, the one card in the folder whose
category is `discussion`.

```yaml
---
metadata:
  category: discussion
  discussion: SLH-D-0099
  opened: "2031-05-17 09:40"
  topic: "One sentence saying what the discussion is for"
  observer: Alice
  token: kiwi
---
```

| Field | Is |
|---|---|
| `category` | `discussion`. It is what makes the card a brief |
| `discussion` | the name of the discussion. It must be the beginning of the folder's name |
| `opened` | the date and the time to the minute, read from a clock, in quotes |
| `topic` | one sentence, in quotes |
| `observer` | the name of the observer. The observer is not asked to return the token |
| `token` | the golden token, as in the folder's name. Absent for the first two discussions, which had none |

### The header of a consolidation

The consolidation is the file `consolidation.hmd`. Its existence closes the
discussion. Its header says how it ended.

```yaml
---
metadata:
  category: consolidation
  discussion: SLH-D-0099
  closed: "2031-05-17 11:05"
  conclusive: true
  returned: [Bob, Carol]
  outcome: "One sentence saying what the discussion came to"
---
```

| Field | Is |
|---|---|
| `category` | `consolidation` |
| `discussion` | the same name as in the brief |
| `closed` | the date and the time to the minute, read from a clock, in quotes |
| `conclusive` | `true` or `false`, written so and not as yes or no |
| `returned` | the workers who returned the golden token. A worker of the folder who is not named here is listed under "Problems" |
| `outcome` | one sentence, in quotes. For a discussion that was not conclusive, it says why |

### An agent's file

A worker's file needs no header. The agent's name is the file's name:
`bob.hmd` is Bob. Every file directly in the folder counts as an agent, so
nothing else belongs there.

## Opening and closing a discussion

For the observer.

**To open one:**

1. Read the clock. Choose a fruit no discussion has used.
2. Make the folder `SLH-D-NNNN-YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM-fruit`, with the next free
   number.
3. Write your file in it, with the header of a brief and the title of your
   name.
4. Render this card. The new row is there, and "Problems" is empty.

**To close one:**

1. See that every worker has returned the token.
2. Write `consolidation.hmd` with its header. Name the workers under
   `returned`, and say honestly whether it was conclusive.
3. Render this card. The row says closed, and "Problems" is empty.

## How it is computed

The functions are in the module `discussions`, at
`.hql/lib/discussions/mod.hql` in the root of the repository. The cells
read `cards`, which is the vault this card lies in: the doc vault.

In VS Code with the HyperMarkDown extension, run a cell from the card. From
a terminal, at the root of the repository:

```sh
hql render doc/discussion/README.hmd
```

This prints the card with the tables in place of the cells. It does not
change the file.

## What it does not show

- **How far each worker is.** That is in the worker's file, in words.
- **Agents that were asked and never wrote.** An agent with no file in the
  folder is not listed.
- **Whether a time is true.** A time that has the right form and was not
  read from a clock is taken as written. For a folder named by the new
  rule, a difference between the name and the header is caught.
- **It does not fail.** The render succeeds whatever "Problems" lists. A
  check that a script can fail on is still to be written.

## How this card came to be

The first version read everything from the two headers. Bob, Carol, Dave
and Percy inspected it in the discussion SLH-D-0004 and found that it was
right on the day and silent on every mistake: a header with one field
missing made its discussion vanish, and one wrong digit showed a closed
discussion open. This version builds the rows from the folders, as Carol
proposed, and adds the table of problems, as Bob and Carol proposed.
